`return_call.wast`/`return_call_indirect.wast` (W11 addendum) close out
the one backlog item W11's own spec explicitly left open: both files
were fetched and read in full while writing that spec, and both parsed
almost entirely with already-supported grammar except for ONE construct,
present in each -- a "Result subtyping" helper declaring `(func $f
(result (ref null $t)) (ref.null $t))`, a NULLABLE reference to a
CONCRETE function type. That's function-references-proposal grammar
distinct from (and much narrower than) the non-null `(ref $t)`/`call_ref`
wall this repo has never had (WASM17's funcref/externref work doesn't
reach it, and this addendum still doesn't). Fixed with a new
`wasm_types::ValueType::ConcreteFuncRef(u32)` variant (a nullable ref
into the FUNC-type index space, the direct analogue of the pre-existing
`StructRef(u32)`'s struct-type index space -- see that variant's own doc
comment for why the two never collide despite sharing a `0x63` binary
tag), `wasm-wast-parser` support for `(ref null $t)` as a value type and
`ref.null $t` as an instruction (both resolving `$t` via the same
`type_names` map every other `(type $t)` reference already uses), and a
`wasm-validator` subtyping rule: a concrete function-type ref IS a
`funcref`, but a bare `funcref` is NOT a specific concrete one -- checked
in BOTH directions by the real corpus (`return_call`/`return_call_
indirect`'s own "Result subtyping" `assert_return` exercises the valid
direction, a sibling `assert_invalid` exercises the mirror-image invalid
one). `wasm-execution`'s bytecode decoder also needed a real fix: its
`ref.null` immediate skip was a hardcoded single byte, which would have
silently corrupted decoding of the new instruction's variable-length
`0x63 <LEB128(idx)>` immediate by treating half of it as the START of
the next instruction.

